      "message": "I think \"verify*\" methods are not generally expected to do side effects like this. My suggestion would be to just return the callback and call startTrackDefaultNetwork in the caller :\n\n\n  setUp() {\n    ...\n    mDefaultNetworkCallback \u003d verifyDNRF()\n    assertNotNull(mDefaultNetworkCallback);\n    mUMN.startTrack()\n    verifyNoMoreInteractions\n  }\n\n  private NetworkCallback verifyDNRF() {\n    captor\n    verify().requestNetwork(capture)\n    verifyNoMoreInteractions()\n    return captor.getValue();\n  }\n\nSure, returning the object from a verify* method is also not standard, but I think in this case it\u0027s very easy to understand even from just the method\u0027s signature. Or, you could rename the method to verifyAndReturn* or getFiledDefaultNetworkRequest.",
